@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Deployment") {
    One static binary and one SQLite file. The release binary is linked statically against musl, so
    the server needs no Swift toolchain and no other service. The database lives at `DATABASE_URL`;
    `serve` listens on `LISTEN_ADDR` and shuts down cleanly on SIGTERM, so it can run as a systemd
    service or in a container.

    ```sh
    rivulet migrate
    rivulet backup /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet backup --verify /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet backup --force /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet vacuum
    rivulet serve
    ```

    `backup` writes a consistent snapshot with SQLite's `VACUUM INTO`. It first checks there is
    room for about twice the database plus its write-ahead log, and refuses to overwrite an existing
    file unless `--force` is given. `--verify` opens the copy and runs an integrity check on it.
    `vacuum` rebuilds the live database to release free pages.

    There is no restore command; restoring is a file swap. Stop `serve`, copy the backup to the
    `DATABASE_URL` path, run `rivulet migrate`, and check the counts with `rivulet status --json`.

    SQLite runs in WAL mode with `synchronous=NORMAL`: a power loss can lose the refresh round that
    was running, but not earlier ones. The log is checkpointed when `serve` stops.

    Schedules are stored as Unix seconds, so a large clock step moves every feed's next check; keep
    the clock synchronised gradually. To upgrade, replace the binary and run `rivulet migrate`; until
    the first release the schema can still change in place, so take a backup first.
  }
}
